   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to commercial\nfood fish licenses",
    "basePrintNo": "S9312",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9312-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13-0328, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9312\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\ncommercial food fish licenses\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend the eligibility limitations for\ncommercial fishing licenses and permits.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill would amend subdivision 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 of §\n13-0328 of the Environmental Conservation Law (ECL) to: 1) maintain a\ncap on the number of commercial food fish, crab, whelk (conch), and\nMarine and Coastal District Party and Charter Boat licenses or permits\nthat may be issued; 2) maintain the current moratorium on new lobster\npermits; and 3) maintain the current eligibility requirements.\n\nSection 2 of this bill provides that the bill shall take effect imme-\ndiately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill would maintain the current license and permit numbers for food\nfish, crab, and whelk. The maximum number of party and charter boat\nlicenses that may be issued to uninspected passenger vessels would\nremain at 517. Further, a complete moratorium would be maintained on\ncommercial lobster permits, given that significant excess harvesting\ncapacity exists in the lobster fishery, and current lobster management\nregulations do not provide for issuance of the required lobster trap\ntags to any new persons. The substantial decline in lobster abundance\nthat has resulted from episodes of mortality beginning in 1999 also\nsupports continuing to prevent new entrants in this troubled fishery.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021-22: Chapter 656 of the Laws of 2021.\n\n2019-20: Chapter 58 of the Laws of 2020.\n\n2019-20: Chapter 21 of the Laws of 2019.\n\n2017-18: Chapter 225 of the Laws of 2017.\n\n \nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act would take effect immediately.\n",
